  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nAnother chilly night ahead across Southern New Mexico and Far \nWest Texas as winds remain very light, allowing temperatures to \ndip into the lower-to-mid 30s. Broken cirrus moving in from the \nwest will keep skies partly cloudy overnight.\n\nQuiet weather on Thanksgiving Day as temperatures remain cool. \nLowland highs in the upper 50s/lower 60s will run slightly below \nnormal for late November, but only by a few degrees. Wind will be\nalmost non-existant. High clouds clearing during the morning hours\nwill be followed by sunny skies in the afternoon and overnight.\nLooks like a great day for holiday festivities.\n\nA warmup is in store to finish the week with light west surface \nwinds 5 to 10 mph and a subtle increase in heights aloft. Lowland \nhighs will warm to the lower-to-mid 60s Friday afternoon, then \nwarm further to the lower 70s on Saturday. \n\n80-knot 500mb jet streak over the Four Corners on Saturday will \nmove down the front of the ridge aloft, crossing through Central \nNew Mexico. Lee surface low formation across the Southern High \nPlains will allow for breezy conditions locally Saturday \nafternoon. West winds 15 to 25 mph with peak gusts to 40 mph is \ncurrently the most likely outcome. Will need to monitor for \nstronger gusts along the east slopes of the Sacramento Mountains, \nBlack Range, and mountain gaps during that timeframe. Forecast is\ncurrently not reaching Wind Advisory criteria, but will be close \nenough to watch over the next few days.\n\nA weak cold front behind this system will knock temperatures back\ndown to normal (lowland highs lower 60s) on Sunday. This looks to\nbe a brief cool down and quick rebound headed into next week with\ndry and zonal flow over the region Monday/Tuesday.\n\nGFS and ECMWF deterministic solutions are in good agreement \nresolving a deeper upper low over the Western U.S. sometime next \nweek (December 2 to 4). This would be our next shot at \nprecipitation chances. Right now, slight chance of lowland rain \nchances and snow chances above 7000 feet AGL is the forecast.",
